video: tegra: fb: take over the firmware framebuffer

JetPack 4's X driver opens /dev/fb0 by name, and its library pairs
/dev/fbN with display head N. The device tree keeps the boot console on
simplefb, which registers first, so tegradc's framebuffer became fb1 and X
sized the screen from simplefb's single mode (800x600).

Give tegra_fb an aperture over the whole address space before it registers.
5.10's framebuffer core then removes the firmware framebuffer it overlaps,
and tegra_fb becomes fb0 and takes the console, as under 4.9.

Found: trial 22. Tested on the Nano: trial 23 logged "fb0: switching to
tegra_fb from simple" and X sized the screen at 1920x1080.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
---
diff --git a/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/fb.c b/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/fb.c
--- a/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/fb.c
+++ b/nvidia/drivers/video/tegra/fb.c
@@ -1396,6 +1396,19 @@ struct tegra_fb_info *tegra_fb_register(struct platform_device *ndev,
 
 	tegra_fb_set_par(info);
 
+	/*
+	 * Take over from a firmware framebuffer (simplefb on the bootloader's
+	 * buffer, which carries the boot console until now) so this one gets
+	 * its number, fb0 for head 0, as under 4.9: JetPack 4's Xorg driver
+	 * opens /dev/fb0 by name, and its libnvdc pairs /dev/fbN with
+	 * /dev/tegra_dc_N.
+	 */
+	info->apertures = alloc_apertures(1);
+	if (info->apertures) {
+		info->apertures->ranges[0].base = 0;
+		info->apertures->ranges[0].size = ~0;
+	}
+
 	if (register_framebuffer(info)) {
 		dev_err(&ndev->dev, "failed to register framebuffer\n");
 		ret = -ENODEV;
